# Introduction to HTML

Before we dive into JavaScript, we need to learn some foundational HTML. We will cover _just enough_ HTML (and CSS) to get us started with JavaScript.

## What is HTML?

**HTML** (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard language for creating web pages. It is a "markup language"---a language for adding **structure** and **formatting** to text. More specifically, it is a markup language for creating "hypertext" documents that can contain links to other documents (which is the foundation of the World Wide Web).

## The Three Web Languages

Web development involves three primary languages:

- **HTML** for defining the structure and content
- **CSS** for specifying how browsers should render/display this content
- **JavaScript** for defining the **behavior** of the page---how it reacts to things like clicks and keypresses

If we use the analogy of a person, **HTML** is like the skeleton (giving structure and shape), **CSS** is like the skin and clothes (giving appearance and style), and **JavaScript** is like the muscles (giving movement and interactivity).

## Using These Notebooks

These notebooks are designed to be interactive. You can run the code cells, edit them, and see the results.
For example, try editing the text in the following cell:

```{html}
<p>Hello! Try "running" this by clicking me and then clicking the "play" button to my left.</p>
```

Some cells will also have tests that you can run by clicking the "play" button to the left of the cell. For example, try editing the cell below to say:

```html
<p>Hello World!</p>
```

```{html}
<p></p>
```
```+solution
<p>Hello World!</p>
```
```+test
assert('p').should('have.text', 'Hello World!').run('Missing or incorrect text', 'Correct text');
```

_Note that punctuation (`!`), capitalization, and spacing matter._


## Creating "Real" HTML Files

These notebooks are meant for learning and experimentation. Your practice exercises will ask you to work with "real" HTML files. To get a sense of how this works, let's create a file manually (for most exercises, you won't need to *create* these from scratch but instead will modify existing ones).


### Create a Directory
First, let's create a folder named `intro-html` inside of the `Module 1 - HTML` folder in the workspace (in VS Code, right-click on the "Module 1 - HTML" folder and select "New Folder").

![VS Code file explorer showing how to create a new folder](images/new_folder.png)

```{external}
title: Create a folder
interval: 2s
- directory: intro-html | Create a folder named `intro-html`
```

Make sure that your directory is in the same directory as this notebook (`Module 1 - HTML`). You can drag and drop it if not.

![VS Code file explorer showing the new folder in the correct location](images/created_intro_html.png)

### Create a File
Next, create a file named `index.html` inside `intro-html`.

The `.html` at the end of that name is the file's **extension**: the part of a file name after the final dot. The extension tells the computer what kind of file it is (in our case, an HTML file is treated as a web page). HTML files are almost always given the `.html` extension (you may occasionally run across the older `.htm`, which means the same thing).

The part before the dot (`index`) is up to you. `index` is typically the default name for the "main" page of a website.

Be careful, though: some computers are set to hide extensions in their file listings, so a file that appears as `index` might really be named `index.html.txt`, which a browser will not treat as a web page. VS Code always shows the full name, so trust the name you see there.

In VS Code, right-click the `intro-html` folder and select "New File", then name the file `index.html`.

```{external}
title: Create an HTML file
interval: 2s
- file: intro-html/index.html | Create a file named `index.html` inside `intro-html`
```

### Add Some HTML
*Note 1: you will want to probably view this page side-by-side with the notebook. In VS Code, you can right-click on the `index.html` file and select "Open to the Side".*

*Note 2: make sure to save your changes by clicking File->Save.*

Open `index.html` and paste the following code into it:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>My First Page</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Hello World!</h1>
  </body>
</html>
```

```{external}
title: Build your first HTML file
interval: 2s
- file: intro-html/index.html | Create a file named `index.html` inside `intro-html`
- contains: intro-html/index.html | <!DOCTYPE html> | Add the HTML doctype to `index.html`
- contains: intro-html/index.html | <title> | Add a `title` element
- contains: intro-html/index.html | <h1>Hello World!</h1> | Add an `h1` heading with the text `Hello World!`
```


### Preview Your Page

To see your page, right-click on `index.html` and select "Show Preview". This will open your page in the browser. You should see your heading displayed.
![A browser window showing the heading "Hello World!"](images/show-preview.png)

Great job! You have created your first HTML page. You can edit the `index.html` file and see your changes reflected in the preview.

### Editing Notebooks vs. "Real" HTML Files

These notebooks are designed to help you learn and experiment with HTML in a "sandbox" environment. Your practice exercises and graded assignments will involve working with "real" HTML files, which you will edit and run in a browser. The skills you learn here will transfer directly to those exercises.

## Exercises

Most of these readings will conclude with a set of short questions. I strongly recommend trying these questions to test and improve your understanding of the material. You can try them as many times as you like and you will *not* be graded on your answers.

Test your understanding of this lesson with the following questions.

```{mcq}
What does HTML stand for?
correct: Correct! HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language, the standard language for creating web pages.
wrong: Not quite. Look back at the first sentence of "What is HTML?" where the full name is spelled out.
[x] HyperText Markup Language
[ ] HyperText Machine Language
[ ] HighText Markdown Language
[ ] HomeText Making Language
```

```{mcq}
What is the main purpose of a "markup language" like HTML?
correct: Exactly! A markup language adds structure and formatting to text. HTML is the skeleton that gives a page its structure and shape.
wrong: Think about the analogy in the lesson: HTML is described as the "skeleton." What does a skeleton provide?
[x] Adding structure and formatting to text
[ ] Styling the colors and fonts a browser displays
[ ] Defining how the page reacts to clicks and keypresses
[ ] Storing data in a database
```

```{mcq}
Based on the lesson's body analogies, select ALL of the matches that are correct.
correct: Correct! HTML is the skeleton (structure), CSS is the skin and clothes (appearance), and JavaScript is the muscles (behavior).
wrong: Not quite. Revisit "The Three Web Languages" section, where each language is compared to a part of the body or its covering.
[x] HTML is the skeleton, providing structure
[x] CSS is the skin and clothes, providing appearance and style
[x] JavaScript is the muscles, providing movement and interactivity
[ ] CSS is the skeleton that defines the page's content
[ ] HTML is the muscles that react to clicks
```

